+---
+
+## Example
+
+Let $\mathcal I$ be the complex finite semisimple fusion category with simple objects $\mathbf 1,\psi,\sigma$, fusion rules $\psi\otimes\psi\cong\mathbf 1$, $\psi\otimes\sigma\cong\sigma\otimes\psi\cong\sigma$, and $\sigma\otimes\sigma\cong\mathbf 1\oplus\psi$, and the coherent Ising associator whose non-identity coefficients are $[F^{\sigma\sigma\sigma}_{\sigma}]_{ab}=2^{-1/2}(-1)^{ab}$ for $a,b\in\{\mathbf 1,\psi\}$, $[F^{\psi\sigma\psi}_{\sigma}]_{\sigma\sigma}=-1$, and $[F^{\sigma\psi\sigma}_{\psi}]_{\sigma\sigma}=-1$. Every other allowed associator coefficient is $1$. The simple object $\sigma$ has no right tensor inverse and is therefore a simple non-invertible object. This is an abstract algebraic example and makes no physical-realization claim.
+
+## Example references
+
+[Simon, *Topological Quantum*, §§9.2–9.3](https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/SteveSimon/topological2020/TopoBookSep12.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** Objects of $\mathcal I$ are finite direct sums of its three simple objects, so any object $E$ has multiplicities $n_{\mathbf 1},n_\psi,n_\sigma\in\mathbb N$.
+
+[F1] [[def-aqft-noninvertible-defect-fusion]]: an object is tensor-invertible only if it has a two-sided tensor inverse.
+
+[F2] [[def-aqft-ising-fusion-data]]: $\sigma\otimes\sigma\cong\mathbf 1\oplus\psi$ and $\sigma\otimes\psi\cong\sigma$.
+
+[F3] [[lem-aqft-ising-fusion-data-coherence]]: these fusion rules and associators define a finite semisimple fusion category, with $\mathbf 1,\psi,\sigma$ simple.
+
+## Verification
+
+1.1 Write an arbitrary object as $E=n_{\mathbf 1}\mathbf 1\oplus n_\psi\psi\oplus n_\sigma\sigma$. Distributivity and [F2] give
+
+$$
+\sigma\otimes E\cong n_\sigma\mathbf 1\oplus n_\sigma\psi\oplus(n_{\mathbf 1}+n_\psi)\sigma.
+$$
+
+If $\sigma\otimes E\cong\mathbf 1$, equality of simple-object multiplicities would require both $n_\sigma=1$ and $n_\sigma=0$ from the $\mathbf 1$ and $\psi$ coefficients. This is impossible. [given, F2, algebra]
+
+1.2 Thus $\sigma$ has no right tensor inverse and cannot have a two-sided tensor inverse by [F1]. By [F3], $\sigma$ is a simple object of $\mathcal I$; it is therefore simple and non-invertible. The argument is purely categorical. [F1, F3, step 1.1] ∎
