+
+## Statement
+
+Fix an integer $L\ge2$ and the periodic site set $\mathbb Z/L\mathbb Z$. Let $\mathcal H_L=\ell^2(\mathbb Z_2^L)$ with orthonormal basis $|h_1,\ldots,h_L\rangle$, where every $h_j\in\mathbb Z_2$ and $h_0=h_L$. Define Pauli operators by $Z_j|\mathbf h\rangle=(-1)^{h_j}|\mathbf h\rangle$ and $X_j|\mathbf h\rangle=|\mathbf h+\mathbf e_j\rangle$. Adopt the finite quantum Ising model with Hamiltonian
+
+$$
+H_L=-J\sum_{j=1}^{L}(Z_jZ_{j+1}+X_j),\qquad J>0,
+$$
+
+where $Z_{L+1}=Z_1$, $J$ has units of energy, and $\hbar=1$. Euclidean propagation through a time interval $s\ge0$ is the exact finite matrix $e^{-sH_L}$, with $s$ in inverse-energy units. Correlators are matrix elements between arbitrary boundary vectors of finite ordered lists of observable matrices and these propagation factors.
+
+Let $\widehat{\mathcal H}_L=\ell^2(\mathbb Z_2^L)$ have basis $|\widehat h_{1/2},\ldots,\widehat h_{L-1/2}\rangle$, with dual Pauli operators $\widehat Z$ and $\widehat X$ defined by the same formulas on half-integer sites. Define the Kramers–Wannier defect map $D_L:\mathcal H_L\to\widehat{\mathcal H}_L$ by
+
+$$
+\langle\widehat{\mathbf h}|D_L|\mathbf h\rangle=2^{-L/2}(-1)^{\sum_{j=1}^{L}\widehat h_{j-1/2}(h_{j-1}+h_j)}.
+$$
+
+Identify the dual chain with the original chain by the half-site shift $j+1/2\mapsto j$; under this identification $D_L$ is an endodefect. A defect line is a wrapping insertion of $D_L$ on a Euclidean time slice. Its allowed position changes are slides within gaps between fixed observable insertions. A finite defect network has labels $\mathbf 1,\psi,\sigma$, represented on the chain by $I,P_L,D_L$, where $P_L=\prod_jX_j$. Its fusion spaces are one-dimensional precisely for
+
+$$
+\mathbf 1\otimes a=a\otimes\mathbf 1=a,\qquad \psi\otimes\psi=\mathbf 1,\qquad \psi\otimes\sigma=\sigma\otimes\psi=\sigma,\qquad \sigma\otimes\sigma=\mathbf 1\oplus\psi,
+$$
+
+and zero otherwise. Fix the Ising junction bases and associator as follows. Write $\epsilon(\mathbf 1)=0$ and $\epsilon(\psi)=1$; then
+
+$$
+[F^{\sigma\sigma\sigma}_{\sigma}]_{ab}=2^{-1/2}(-1)^{\epsilon(a)\epsilon(b)},\qquad a,b\in\{\mathbf 1,\psi\},
+$$
+
+$$
+[F^{\psi\sigma\psi}_{\sigma}]_{\sigma\sigma}=-1,\qquad [F^{\sigma\psi\sigma}_{\psi}]_{\sigma\sigma}=-1,
+$$
+
+and every other allowed associator coefficient is $1$. In the local height basis, the nontrivial trivalent tensors for two duality lines and one spin-flip line are $2^{-1/4}(-1)^h$ and $(X)_{hh'}=\delta_{h',h+1}$; reversed orientations use adjoint tensors. These data define all local junctions and re-association moves in the finite network model.
+
+For this selected finite model, $D_L$ is topological under the declared defect slides, its exact operator fusion is $D_L^2=I+P_L$, $P_L^2=I$, and $D_LP_L=P_LD_L=D_L$, and the junction spaces and associators are the finite semisimple unitary Ising fusion data above. In particular $D_L$ is non-invertible. The claim is confined to this finite lattice model and asserts no continuum limit or empirical fact.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[Aasen, Mong, and Fendley, §§2–4](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1601.07185)
+
+[Fröhlich, Fuchs, Runkel, and Schweigert, critical-Ising defect discussion](https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/0404051)
+
+[Simon, *Topological Quantum*, §§9.2–9.3](https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/SteveSimon/topological2020/TopoBookSep12.pdf)
+
+[Carqueville, *Lecture Notes on 2-Dimensional Defect TQFT*, §2.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1607.05747)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** Adopt the finite periodic Hamiltonian, Euclidean matrix-element correlators, duality kernel, defect insertion rule and local junction data in the statement. All spin and dual-spin labels lie in $\mathbb Z_2$; $J>0$ has energy units; $s$ has inverse-energy units; and there is no empirical premise.
+
+[A1] This is an adopted finite-lattice model, not a claim that nature or a continuum QFT realizes the model.
+
+[F1] [[def-aqft-topological-defect]]: in a specified model, a defect is topological when every declared correlator is invariant under every allowed isotopy that preserves the other insertions and boundary data.
+
+[F2] [[def-aqft-noninvertible-defect-fusion]]: an object is tensor-invertible only if it has a two-sided fusion inverse; fusion data include junction maps and associators, not only fusion multiplicities.
+
+[F3] [[def-aqft-ising-fusion-data]]: the Ising fusion data have labels $\mathbf 1,\psi,\sigma$, the displayed Ising fusion table, and the displayed associator and duality coefficients.
+
+[F4] [[lem-aqft-ising-fusion-data-coherence]]: these data satisfy the monoidal coherence and duality identities and define a finite semisimple unitary fusion category.
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 In the kernel formula, toggling the input height $h_j$ changes the exponent by $\widehat h_{j-1/2}+\widehat h_{j+1/2}$, while multiplying the input by $Z_jZ_{j+1}$ contributes $(-1)^{h_j+h_{j+1}}$, which is exactly the exponent change from toggling the output dual height $\widehat h_{j+1/2}$. Thus
+
+$$
+D_LX_j=\widehat Z_{j-1/2}\widehat Z_{j+1/2}D_L,\qquad D_LZ_jZ_{j+1}=\widehat X_{j+1/2}D_L.
+$$
+
+Summing these identities gives $D_LH_L=\widehat H_LD_L$, where $\widehat H_L=-J\sum_j(\widehat Z_{j-1/2}\widehat Z_{j+1/2}+\widehat X_{j+1/2})$. The half-site identification makes $\widehat H_L=H_L$. [A1, given, algebra]
+
+1.2 Induction gives $D_LH_L^n=H_L^nD_L$ for every $n\ge0$. The finite-dimensional exponential series converges absolutely, so termwise equality yields $D_Le^{-sH_L}=e^{-sH_L}D_L$ for every $s\ge0$. [A1, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+2.1 If the defect time $\tau$ moves inside a gap $(t_a,t_{a+1})$ with no observable insertion, the two adjacent propagators combine as
+
+$$
+e^{-(t_{a+1}-\tau)H_L}D_Le^{-(\tau-t_a)H_L}=D_Le^{-(t_{a+1}-t_a)H_L},
+$$
+
+by [step 1.2] and the semigroup law. All other factors in the correlator stay fixed, so its matrix element is independent of $\tau$. Thus every declared line-slide preserves the correlator. [A1, F1, step 1.2, algebra]
+
+2.2 Composing two kernels and summing over the intermediate dual heights gives
+
+$$
+\langle\mathbf h'|D_L^2|\mathbf h\rangle=2^{-L}\sum_{\widehat{\mathbf h}\in\mathbb Z_2^L}(-1)^{\sum_j\widehat h_{j-1/2}(h_{j-1}+h_j+h'_{j-1}+h'_j)}=\prod_j\delta_{d_{j-1},d_j},
+$$
+
+where $d_j=h_j+h'_j$ in $\mathbb Z_2$ and each sum $\sum_{u\in\mathbb Z_2}(-1)^{ur}$ equals $2$ if $r=0$ and $0$ if $r=1$. The constraints force all $d_j$ to be the same bit, so $\mathbf h'=\mathbf h$ or $\mathbf h'=\mathbf h+\mathbf 1$. These are exactly the matrix elements of $I+P_L$, proving $D_L^2=I+P_L$. [A1, given, algebra]
+
+3.1 Toggling every input height leaves the kernel unchanged because each neighboring pair changes by $1+1=0$ in $\mathbb Z_2$; shifting every output dual height leaves it unchanged because $\sum_j(h_{j-1}+h_j)=0$ in $\mathbb Z_2$. Hence $D_LP_L=P_LD_L=D_L$, while $P_L^2=I$ by $X_j^2=I$. The fusion operators therefore satisfy the stated Ising fusion table. [A1, step 2.2, algebra]
+
+3.2 The network's junction spaces and local re-association maps are exactly the finite data in [F3]. The lattice tensors give the displayed $F$ coefficients by the two-state internal-height contraction; the only two-channel change of basis is $2^{-1/2}\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&-1\end{pmatrix}$, and the mixed one-channel moves are the stated $-1$ signs. By [F4], these maps satisfy pentagon and both duality identities, so the declared re-association moves are consistent for every finite network. [A1, F3, F4, step 3.1, algebra]
+
+3.3 Since $P_L$ is nontrivial (it sends $|0,\ldots,0\rangle$ to $|1,\ldots,1\rangle$), the relation $D_LP_L=D_L$ would imply $P_L=I$ if $D_L$ had a left inverse, a contradiction. Thus $D_L$ has no two-sided inverse. Steps [2.1], [3.1], and [3.2] establish topologicality, operator fusion, and coherent junction data for the finite model; this is a conditional lattice-model deduction, not an empirical or continuum claim. [A1, F2, F4, step 2.1, step 3.1, step 3.2] ∎
+
+## Remarks
+
+The square records the exact local intertwiner used in step 1.1. Its commutativity follows from the displayed kernel calculation; no proof relies on the diagram.
+
+```tikzcd
+\mathcal H_L \arrow[r,"D_L"] \arrow[d,"Z_jZ_{j+1}"'] & \widehat{\mathcal H}_L \arrow[d,"\widehat X_{j+1/2}"] \\
+\mathcal H_L \arrow[r,"D_L"'] & \widehat{\mathcal H}_L
+```
